WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.700
Narrator: Imagine that you're
a red-blooded carnivore.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.060
You love burgers, steak,
pork chops, bacon.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.370
But one day, out of nowhere,
red meat starts to make

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:11.680
you physically sick to the stomach.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.700
It sounds like science
fiction, but it's real,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.783
and it's spreading.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.340
It's spreading to people like Amy.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.740
Amy: My name is Amy Pearl,
and I'm a producer for WNYC.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:24.810
Narrator: She has what is called

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.210
a mammalian meat allergy.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.010
Amy: I have a tendency to not
mention it at restaurants,

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:32.459
because I feel like if
you say to a server,

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:35.340
I'm allergic to meat,
they're gonna be like,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:36.800
I'm spitting in your food.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.830
Narrator: Any meat that came from a cow,

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.468
a pig, or a lamb, will make Amy sick.

00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:44.200
Very, very sick.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.270
Amy: Like I just had hives
on my hands and my feet,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.850
and like all over my torso.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:53.460
I was nauseous, and I
felt like I was fainting,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.100
I felt like the world was ending,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.790
I felt like I was gonna pass out

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.390
and I couldn't really breathe.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.870
Narrator: Thousands of Americans
are suffering like Amy,

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.700
but until 2009, this sort
of allergy went undiagnosed.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.620
Amy: I think I made an appointment

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.020
with my regular physician,
but he immediately was like,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.960
there's no such thing as a meat allergy,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.470
has to be something else.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.710
Narrator: That changed with
the cancer drug, Cetuximab.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:19.020
In a clinical trial, one
in four patients developed

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.450
severe allergic reactions to the drug.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.302
Some even died.

00:01:23.302 --> 00:01:26.190
Naturally, Cetuximab was investigated.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.620
University of Virginia's
allergy department focused

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.100
on one specific part of the drug.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.100
The key ingredient in Cetuximab
is a specific carbohydrate

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.840
that all non-primate mammals
carry in their cell walls

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.000
and tissues,
Galactose-alpha-1,3-galactose,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.050
or, if you're pressed for time, alpha-gal.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.120
Dogs have it, cats have
it, and the mice cells

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.793
involved in the production
of Cetuximab have alpha-gal.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.170
The team discovered that
those who had reactions

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:58.043
were from only certain areas
of the US, the southeast.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.920
The locations of the cases
aligned almost perfectly

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.520
with the range of a specific type of tick,

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:05.813
the lone star tick.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.030
Dr. Scott Commins is an allergist,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.160
and was working with the
University at the time.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.530
Scott: Over 90 to 93% of our patients

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.380
that developed allergic
reactions to red meat

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.740
and test positive by blood test will have

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.120
a history of tick bites.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.470
Amy: The thing I Googled
was "sudden meat allergy."

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.670
I found an article that
said there was some man

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.790
in Florida, had gone
into anaphylactic shock

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.310
from eating meat after a tick bite.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.840
And I was like, "I had a tick bite!"

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.530
I mean, I often have a tick bite.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.180
I'd just taken a tick off me.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.600
Narrator: One of the leading researchers,

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.860
Dr. Thomas Platts-Mills,
went so far to use himself

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.354
in an unofficial experiment,
taking a hike through

00:02:48.354 --> 00:02:50.500
a nest of larval ticks.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.400
It earned him a nice
case of red meat allergy.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.370
There's still a lot we
don't know about how

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:59.383
this meat allergy works, but
here is the leading theory.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.600
Ticks don't have alpha-gal naturally,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.930
but they could be carrying
it if they fed off a mammal,

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.930
like a deer or a dog.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.350
If a tick then bites you, it trades some

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.940
of your delicious blood for its saliva,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.600
which is a cocktail of nasty things.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.280
An enzyme in that saliva
tells your body that there's

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.260
a variety of dangerous threats,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.723
and your immune system bans
everything in that saliva

00:03:21.723 --> 00:03:25.640
from entering the body,
including alpha-gal,

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.290
which is also in every burger,
steak, and bacon strip.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.640
So the next time you eat one of those,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.420
your body treats the
carbohydrate like an intruder,

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.040
and hits the panic button.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.560
This is happening in the bodies

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.568
of an estimated 5,000 Americans.

00:03:42.568 --> 00:03:44.450
What's worse is that the range

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.990
of the lone star tick is growing.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.450
Scott: Their range is spreading into

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.750
the Ohio River Valley and
now up into Minnesota.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.290
We also know places where this
alpha-gal red meat allergy

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:59.890
exists, but they don't have
lone star ticks at all.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.610
And this would be southern
Sweden, for example,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.800
there are parts of Europe, Australia,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.620
and now even South Africa.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.330
So clearly other tick
species can do this as well.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.040
Narrator: University of
Virginia's researchers

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.160
have also linked the alpha-gal allergies

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.480
with a higher risk of heart disease.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.640
Scott: This allergy seems
as though it will often

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.400
go away over time, but
the problem has been

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.490
that any additional tick bites seem

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.920
to cause the allergy to return.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.390
And these are often patients
who like to be outside.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.780
Amy: I know that my
numbers have gone down,

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.990
because I've been retested
a couple of times,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.150
but they're still 10, 20
times what they should be.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.870
Narrator: Dr. Commins
continues to work towards

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.090
an immediate cure to
mammalian meat allergy.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:47.650
In the meantime, the
number of cases are rising.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.020
Scott: So what we've
been trying to do is work

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.520
on a vaccine related to tick saliva,

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.134
in hopes that we can prevent
the allergic response

00:04:56.134 --> 00:05:00.570
from continuing, or recurring,
with additional tick bites.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.830
Narrator: If you've been
bitten by ticks recently,

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.310
be sure to get tested.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:07.670
If you haven't, learn how
to explore the woods safely.

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:08.860
Scott: Well if you haven't been bitten,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:11.130
then try to stick to the trails.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.610
If you're going into a
heavy tick-infested area,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.500
you may want to consider
pre-treating your skin

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.050
or clothing with DEET or
Permethrin, respectively.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.730
Amy: People are so
freaked out about ticks,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.100
it's not that bad.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.086
They're much easier to see than you think.

00:05:28.086 --> 00:05:29.180
Narrator: Learn how to do a tick check

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.630
after spending time in the wilderness.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:35.290
And if you value a juicy
steak over a walk in nature,

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.573
then maybe stay out of the woods.

